1 BACKUP

   Works with VMS BACKUP save sets: lists a save set, restores files from
   one, compares one with the files it was made from, and saves files
   into a new one.  A save set may be a file on a mounted volume, as VMS
   keeps one, or a host file holding the same bytes (one copied from a
   VAX by binary FTP, or with COPY/BINARY).

   Format

     BACKUP input-specifier output-specifier

   The four operations are:

     BACKUP/LIST[=filespec] save-set/SAVE_SET
     BACKUP save-set/SAVE_SET output-specifier
     BACKUP/COMPARE save-set/SAVE_SET filespec
     BACKUP filespec[,...] save-set/SAVE_SET

2 Parameters

 input-specifier

   The files to save, as a list of file specifications, or the save set
   to list, restore, or compare, with /SAVE_SET after it.

 output-specifier

   The save set to write, with /SAVE_SET after it; or for a restore, the
   directory the files go to (see Description); or for a compare, the
   files to compare the save set's with.  /LIST alone needs none.

2 Description

   BACKUP follows VMS V7.1's BACKUP V6.2: the save sets it writes are
   laid out as that version lays them out, and VMS reads them.  Image,
   physical, and incremental operations, tapes, and copies of files to
   files aren't done.

   A save set on a disk must have /SAVE_SET, and which specifier is the
   save set says what BACKUP does: as the input, a restore (or with
   /COMPARE, a compare); as the output, a save.  /LIST alone lists the
   input save set.  Where a qualifier stands says what it is about, as
   in VMS: after BACKUP it is a command qualifier; after the input
   specifier, an input save-set or input file-selection qualifier; after
   the output specifier, an output save-set or output file qualifier.

   The output specifier of a restore names the directory the files go
   to: [*...] restores each file to the directory it was saved from,
   [dir...] to a tree under [dir], and [dir] to that one directory (no
   directory, or [], is the default directory).  Under [dir...] a file
   keeps its whole directory ([B69.SUB1]A.TXT goes to [dir.B69.SUB1]),
   or with /SELECT the part the selection's "..." matched:
   /SELECT=[B69...] restores [B69.SUB1]A.TXT to [dir.SUB1].  A name or
   type in the output specifier renames the files; each keeps its saved
   version.  Directories that don't exist are made.  A host directory
   may be the output too ("out", or "out/..." for a tree); its files are
   written as COPY writes them.

   A save goes through each directory in order, every version of each
   file unless the specifier names one, and with [dir...] into each
   subdirectory, which is saved too, where its directory file is.  Each
   element of an input list starts in a new save set block, and takes
   the device and directory of the element before it when it names
   none.

   A restored file keeps its saved attributes: record format, dates,
   revision count, protection, characteristics, version limit,
   allocation, and access control list.  A file the save set has only
   part of is restored with zeros for the blocks missing (VBNMISSING).

2 Qualifiers
/BACKUP

   Input file-selection: /SINCE and /BEFORE look at each file's backup
   date.

/BEFORE

      /BEFORE=time

   Input file-selection: saves only files dated before the time (TODAY,
   YESTERDAY, TOMORROW, or a VMS time), by the date /BACKUP, /CREATED,
   /EXPIRED, or /MODIFIED (the default) names.

/BLOCK_SIZE

      /BLOCK_SIZE=n

   Output save set: the save set's block size in bytes, 2048 to 65535,
   rounded up to a multiple of 512.  The default is 32256.

/BRIEF

   Command: a listing shows each file's name, used blocks, and creation
   date.  This is the default.

/BY_OWNER

      /BY_OWNER[=option]

   Input file-selection: saves only files with this owner, a UIC (yours
   if none is given).

   Output save set: the save set file's owner, a UIC.

   Output file (a restore): the restored files' owner: DEFAULT (yours,
   the default without /BY_OWNER), ORIGINAL (the saved owner, the
   default with /BY_OWNER alone), PARENT (the directory's), or a UIC.

/COMMENT

      /COMMENT=string

   Output save set: puts the text in the save set's summary.

/COMPARE

   Command: compares the save set's files with the files named.

/CRC

   Input save set: checks each block's CRC, recovering a damaged block
   from its redundancy group.  As in VMS V7.1, the default is /NOCRC; a
   block whose header is damaged is recovered either way.

   Output save set: writes each block's CRC.  The default is /NOCRC.

/CREATED

   Input file-selection: /SINCE and /BEFORE look at each file's creation
   date.

/EXCLUDE

      /EXCLUDE=(filespec[,...])

   Input save set: leaves out the save set's files these names match.

   Input file-selection: leaves out the files these names match.  A
   directory file is never left out.

/EXPIRED

   Input file-selection: /SINCE and /BEFORE look at each file's
   expiration date.

/FULL

   Command: a listing shows a block for each file, with its dates,
   owner, file ID, protection, and record attributes.

/GROUP_SIZE

      /GROUP_SIZE=n

   Output save set: the blocks in each redundancy group.  The default is
   10, and 0 writes none.

/HOST

   Input or output save set: the save set is a host file, whatever its
   name.

/IGNORE

      /IGNORE=(option[,...])

   Command: NOBACKUP saves the data of files marked NOBACKUP too, of
   which only the header is saved otherwise.  INTERLOCK saves a file
   another process has open for writing, as it stands; otherwise such a
   file isn't saved.

/LIST

      /LIST[=filespec]

   Command: lists the save set on SYS$OUTPUT, or into the file (type
   .LIS unless it gives one).  As in VMS V7.1, a listing shows every
   file: /SELECT and /EXCLUDE don't change it.

/LOG

   Command: shows each file as it is saved, restored, or compared.

/MODIFIED

   Input file-selection: /SINCE and /BEFORE look at each file's revision
   date.  This is the default.

/NEW_VERSION

   Output file (a restore): restores a file that exists as a new
   version.  With none of /NEW_VERSION, /OVERLAY, and /REPLACE, a file
   that exists isn't restored.

/OVERLAY

   Output file (a restore): writes over a file that exists, in place.

/REPLACE

   Output file (a restore): deletes a file that exists, and restores it
   again.

/SAVE_SET

   Input or output: the specifier is a save set.

/SELECT

      /SELECT=(filespec[,...])

   Input save set: restores or compares only the save set's files these
   names match.

/SINCE

      /SINCE=time

   Input file-selection: saves only files dated since the time, as
   /BEFORE takes it.

2 Examples

   1.$ BACKUP/LIST DUA1:[SETS]B69.BCK/SAVE_SET

     Lists the save set B69.BCK.

   2.$ BACKUP/LOG DUA1:[SETS]B69.BCK/SAVE_SET/SELECT=[B69]*.TXT [R1...]

     Restores the save set's .TXT files from [B69] to a tree under [R1].

   3.$ BACKUP/LIST "./pi.bck"/SAVE_SET

     Lists a save set that is a host file.

   4.$ BACKUP [WORK...]*.* DUA1:[SETS]WORK.BCK/SAVE_SET

     Saves [WORK] and everything below it in WORK.BCK.
